esolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineGrammar.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/DataTreeReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeAlignment.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeSummary.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ParseHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptParamDefs.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/BestPracticesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/DebugDataMismatch.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/OptimizeCanvas.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Resources/ResourceRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Outline.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Values.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/changes/canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "gh_inspect status: the type-name fallback for an empty nickname has no test and no live-check step", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Commit 819e314 changed what status returns in `name` (GhInspectTool.cs line 267: `string.IsNullOrEmpty(obj.NickName) ? obj.Name : obj.NickName`; it was the nickname, empty or not). Nothing would fail if that line regressed: GhInspectTool.cs is host-coupled and not linked into Cordyceps.Tests, no test file mentions the fallback (ObjectHealthTests covers the population and the classification only), and no VRF-021 step asks for the `name` of an unnamed slider or panel (step 14 checks status and shape, not the value). The rest of ActionStatus's host behaviour has one or the other; this has neither. It broke because the choice was written inline in the tool file. This is the case the learnings rule under 'Validate-then-mutate, and never conflate \"unparseable\" with \"empty\"' names: 'New pure decision logic goes in a host-free `Core/` file linked into the test project WITH tests in the same chunk, even when it feels like host glue'. Close it by moving the choice into a host-free helper beside ObjectHealth (nickname, type name -> name) with tests for a null, an empty and a set nickname, and have ActionStatus call it. The defect itself is small (an empty `name` on a status entry whose `id` is still right); the rating is the protocol's for changed behaviour with no coverage of any kind.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Printable-name rule misses a separator that forms across both edges of a one-character name", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to four names. OutlineGrammar.IsPrintableCore tests `name + neighbour` and `neighbour + name` separately, so it never sees a separator made of the space before the name, the whole name, and the space after it. The nicknames `\u00b7`, `\u2190`, `\u2192` and `=` therefore pass (traced by hand: for `\u00b7` every `after` hit starts at index 1 = name.Length and every `before` hit ends at neighbour.Length, so nothing is refused), and DisplayNames prints them. In an inline list the name then is the separator: a group line built by OutlineView.GroupLine reads `in \u2190 \u00b7 \u00b7 out: \u2026`, which splits on ` \u00b7 ` into `in \u2190` and `\u00b7 out: \u2026` and loses the name; `out: = (4) \u2192 OUTPUT` and `inputs: = = 4` show a value separator where the name is. That contradicts the rule as the guide, the help tip and the plan state it (no separator may form where the name meets what is printed beside it), and an agent reading such a line passes the wrong token as an id. Who is affected is narrow: a user who nicknamed an object exactly one of those four characters. The single-edge cases 819e314 added are sound and pinned: the rows `a,`, `a;`, `(m)` and `= x` in Printable_RefusesNamesThatCannotBeCutOut fail against the previous rule. Close the class by construction rather than by listing the four names: for every pair of neighbours test `left + name + right`, and refuse when any separator occurrence overlaps the name's span other than starting exactly at its end or ending exactly at its start; build the neighbour set from Separators plus the single space so the two lists cannot drift. Add `\u00b7`, `\u2190`, `\u2192` and `=` to the refused rows.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineGrammar.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Outline's separators have two definitions: OutlineGrammar's list guards names, OutlineView prints most of them as its own literals", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs. Why it broke: the printable-name rule was added as a list beside the view, and only the lines chunk 07 rewrote were pointed at it. OutlineGrammar's summary says 'OutlineView builds its lines from these constants', but the view references only Field, Item, Tags, TagsClose and Value. Entry, FedFrom, Feeds and Detail are referenced nowhere outside OutlineGrammar.cs: the view writes \"; \" (line 742), \"\u2190 \" and \"in \u2190 \" (599, 740), \" \u2192 \" (872), \" (\" (488, 495, 497, 595, 620, 726), \" = \" (772) and five string.Join(\" \u00b7 \", ...) calls (512, 743, 759, 802, 892) as literals. IsPrintable, which carries the guarantee that every printed name can be cut back out of its line, therefore checks a hand-kept mirror of what the view prints. Consequence: a maintainer who adds or changes a separator at one of the literal sites has nothing telling them the Separators list must follow, and the class summary tells them the opposite; a nickname containing the new sequence then prints as a name that cannot be cut out, and the round-trip tests only cover the names in their fixture. The same class already produced one defect in this bundle (names ending ',' or ';', fixed in 819e314). Close it by construction: have every separator the view prints beside a name come from the OutlineGrammar constants (the golden tests pin the output byte for byte, so the swap is checkable without a host), build Neighbours from Separators plus the single space instead of repeating the eight names, and drop the IsPrintable -> IsPrintableCore pass-through, which adds a layer and no behaviour. If the literals stay, delete the summary's claim that the view is built from the constants. To find members: search OutlineView.cs for string literals containing \u00b7, \u2190, \u2192, \"; \", \" (\" or \" = \".", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineGrammar.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Destructive intent is opt-in on the single lookups, which three of the four removal actions use", "recommendation": "Scope: class (every action that removes something from the canvas; search the dispatch switches of the tool classes for remove, delete, clear and rebuild paths). Why it is open: the many-id helper (ToolHelpers.ResolveIds) takes ResolveIntent with no default, but the single lookups (TryGetUnprotectedComponent, TryGetUnprotectedComponentWithDoc, TryGetUnprotectedObject, TryGetUnprotectedGroup) each keep an overload without it that means Ordinary, and group_delete, gh_wire disconnect and gh_wire clear all go through those. Today's five call sites are right, and the plan records the shape as an accepted mid-build decision, so no edit is asked for before merge. What the owner should know: the rule the owner called 'too dangerous' to get wrong holds at the tool layer by five hand-placed arguments in host-coupled code no unit test reaches; a later removal action written against the shorter overload resolves a unique default nickname with no compile error and no failing test. The plan already names the next candidates (zoomable remove and set_count, the script parameter rebuild) as waiting on the owner. If that call comes back 'destructive', a host-free table of action -> intent that dispatch reads, with a test pinning the destructive set against each tool's ActionInfo, would make the list one declared thing rather than scattered arguments.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The default group name is written twice: ObjectEntry.DefaultGroupName and the literal group_add assigns", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the constant was added for the refusal rule and the place that hands the name out was not pointed at it. GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs lines 123-124 assign groupName ?? \"Group\"; ObjectEntry.DefaultGroupName (\"Group\") is what group_delete refuses and what outline declines to print, and its doc comment records the link only in prose ('group_add without a name uses the same text'). If the two drift, an unnamed group made by Cordyceps itself stops being treated as default-named. Use ObjectEntry.DefaultGroupName at both assignments.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "The removal refusal teaches rename as a remedy while the server instructions and rename's own help say not to rename", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (one message family and the documents that repeat it). Why: the refusal text was written to offer two ways forward without being read against the standing guidance. ObjectResolver.DefaultNameRefusal ends '... or name it first with gh_canvas(action='rename', id='...', nickname='NEW') and pass that name', and the delete, gh_wire disconnect and gh_wire clear tips, CHANGELOG.md, README.md, GettingStartedGuide.md, CommonErrorsGuide.md and CanvasOutlineGuide.md repeat 'or rename the object first'. GetServerInstructions() still says 'do NOT rename components while building ... Track components by the returned id', rename's tip says 'only rename when the user explicitly asks for it', and BestPracticesGuide.md says the same. The id is offered first, so the likely cost is small: an agent refused on gh_wire clear or disconnect may rename a user's component only to remove a wire, where the id the error prints needs no change to the canvas. For delete the rename is pointless, since the object is removed. This is the owner's wording to settle: either drop the rename half from the object refusal (the id is precise and sufficient; group_rename is a different case, because naming groups is the recommended practice), or leave it and accept the tension on the record.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "rule-unenforced: CLAUDE.md's Documentation Audit has no check, and the guides' outline examples and quoted error texts are hand copies of what the tests pin", "recommendation": "Scope: class (user-facing prose that restates output the code produces; search CHANGELOG.md, README.md, src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/**/*.md, GetServerInstructions() and the ActionInfo tips for quoted output lines and enumerated rules). The rule: CLAUDE.md 'Documentation Audit (MANDATORY)' - 'Every change to Cordyceps must include an audit of user-facing documentation'. No record-lint check, hook or test owns it (record_lint's checks cover plan refs, norms, suite totals, learnings budgets, fragments and Retires: phrases, none of which reads a guide against code). State today, checked by reading: the three JSON examples in CanvasOutlineGuide.md equal GoldenOverview, GoldenGroup and GoldenAround in CanvasOutlineTests.cs line for line; every quoted refusal and ambiguity text in CHANGELOG.md, README.md, CommonErrorsGuide.md and VRF-019 equals what ObjectResolver builds; no sentence anywhere still says a default nickname resolves on a removal action, that status reads components only, or shows an old-grammar outline line. The instances this finding stands in for, each of which changes nothing an agent does: (1) 819e314 widened the printable rule (a name ending ',' or ';', or starting '(' or '= ', is now printed by id) and updated the guide, but CHANGELOG.md's outline entry still lists only whitespace, a line break, a leading '[' or '+' and a separator inside the name, and the outline help tip lists the trailing ',' and ';' but not the leading forms; (2) GettingStartedGuide.md says 'Every line starts with a name you can pass back as an id', while ungrouped, networks and unconnected start with a count or the word network. The earlier cumulative review found five more of the same kind, so the class recurs. What would end it: link the guide markdown into the test project and assert that each fenced JSON block in CanvasOutlineGuide.md equals the matching golden constant, and that each error text CommonErrorsGuide.md quotes equals ObjectResolver's output for a fixture; for enumerated rules such as the printable list, make the shorter copies relational ('see gh://docs/canvas-outline for the full rule') rather than restating the list.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Outline.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "A shipped doc comment cites the build plan's contract id", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the comment was written while the plan was open on the desk. The summary of ToolHelpers.TryGetUnprotectedObject(GrasshopperContext, ...) ends 'A nickname never matches a group (C1 scopes).' C1 is a section of .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-canvas-read.md and means nothing once the plan is archived. Delete the parenthesis; the sentence before it already states the rule, and ResolveScope's own summary is the durable home. It is the only such citation in the bundle's shipped code.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Ruling 6 ships on two inferences the owner has not confirmed: which actions count as removal, and which names count as default", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to the inferences under the plan's `## Goals`. Why it matters: owner ruling 9 is 'open the PR and merge when ready' and the project merges automatically, so anything still marked inferred merges without the owner having seen it as a question. The plan does separate rulings 1-9 from inferences (each is tagged `*Inferred from ruling N*`), so this is not a marking defect; it is that the marks are still open at the merge boundary. (a) The removal list. The plan infers 'destructive' = `delete`, `group_delete`, `gh_wire clear`, `gh_wire disconnect`, and excludes only 'actions that change a value or a position'. Searching by behaviour rather than by the helper's callers (learning: 'Tracing consumers of a shared helper misses paths that bypass it') finds three more removal sites that still take a unique default nickname: `group_remove` (`group.RemoveObject`, GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs:178), `zoomable` remove/set_count and the `gh_script` parameter rebuild (`UnregisterInputParameter`/`UnregisterOutputParameter`, which also drop the port's wires). The port cases are recorded as open (plan mid-build decisions, the fragment's 'Open for the owner', issue #107); `group_remove` is named nowhere, and it is neither a value nor a position change. Meanwhile CHANGELOG.md, the server instructions and the fragment's Decisions paragraph present the four-action list as 'the actions that remove something', and the fragment tags that sentence '(owner ruling, second set)', so a reader takes the list as the owner's and as complete. Who does what wrong: an agent that reads 'removal actions refuse default nicknames' passes `Circle` to `zoomable` remove or `group_remove` expecting the guard, and it acts. (b) What counts as default. `CanvasReader.IsDefaultNickName` treats a nickname as default when it equals the proxy's nickname OR the object's type name; the plan records only the proxy half (Requirements Confidence) and the group half ('exactly `Group`, to confirm live'). The type-name half now decides what `delete` refuses and makes the error say 'which every new X gets' about a name a user may have typed; it is recorded only in a code comment. (c) Also still marked inferred: status entries for parameter objects stay under `components` (ruling 7), the separator choice and printing an id for an unprintable nickname (ruling 8), and the four first-wave inferences in the `Inferences` list. To close: put (a) and (b) to the owner as explicit questions in the PR description alongside #107, and record the answer in the plan as rulings 6-8 were; until then, have the fragment's Decisions sentence say the four-action list is the builder's reading of ruling 6, and record the type-name rule and its reason in the plan's decisions. No code change is implied unless the owner widens the list.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs", "CHANGELOG.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: VRF-019 steps 26-32 hard-code `Circle` as a fresh Circle's nickname, which depends on a host display setting", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (VRF-019 steps 26-32 and the 'Canvas for the checks' sentence). Rated NOTE because the host fact cannot be settled from this repository: the reader counts both the proxy nickname and the type name as default, which suggests a new Curve/Circle carries the proxy's short nickname (`Cir` in stock Grasshopper) unless full names are being drawn. If the operator's Circle arrives as `Cir`, step 26's `info id='Circle'` answers `No object named 'Circle'.` and reads as a failure of ordinary resolution, and the refusal texts quoted in steps 27-31 never appear as written. Make the steps relational: have step 26 first read the new object's nickname (`gh_canvas(action='info', id='<its id>')`) and use that text wherever the steps say `Circle`. Everything else in the three oracle files was checked against the code and holds: the single and plural refusal texts (ObjectResolver.Describe), the `Target:` prefix on disconnect and its absence on clear, `rename ... nickname=` / `group_rename ... name=` in the taught calls, step ordering (POSTS is deleted only in step 17 and step 22 is marked to run before it; C1 and R survive for VRF-020 step 11; the group-`Group` check runs after POSTS and TMP are gone), and VRF-021 steps 14, 17 and 18 (status and outline both count through `ObjectHealth.IsSubject` and the one `Classify` rule, so error, warning and disconnected counts agree while totals differ). No step depends on something an earlier step removed.", "files": [".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions in the canvas-read plan, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none (a mandated listing; no defect). Decisions the plan records as made mid-build, and what settled each: (1) tree's `object` is the nickname and `id` the GUID, no DisplayName - settled by label-versus-handle reasoning after the Chunk 03 review; shown to the owner in the PR. (2) Chunk 03's refinements to C3 - Product goal (a result never says less than is true). (3) `gh_inspect log limit=0` means the default 100 - consequence of the sentinel fix; announced in CHANGELOG. (4) Chunk 01's refinements to C1 (group short ids in both scopes, entries counted from 0, prefix hint needs a digit) - Product goal (every printed name round-trips). (5) Chunk 02's departures from 'a full GUID behaves as today' - C1 and the Product goal. (6) Chunk 04's refinements to C2, with the coordinator turning `depth` on a group id into an error - Product goal. (7) tree no longer trims ids; a test written on this branch that pinned trimming was corrected - C1 (text is never trimmed). (8) the first cumulative review's warnings answered in one pass, narrowing 'ids are not scoped' so a group's id reaches only group-capable actions - Product goal. (9) left as is: two owners of the full-GUID reading and of repeat marking (#96, #105); `unread` can double-count. (10) Chunks 06-07 as built: intent is a required argument for many-id resolution, 'Did you mean' never offers a default name under destructive intent, default group name taken as exactly `Group`, printable-name rule and its edge cases, status walks active objects so phantoms drop out, a group named `Group` prints by id - owner rulings 6-8 and the Product goal. (11) status falls back to the type name for an empty nickname - review of 06/07. (12) port removal as destructive - not decided, put to the owner (#107). Alignment-pass misses: three first-wave positions were overturned once the owner saw the build - default nicknames resolving on removal actions (ruling 6), status reading components only (ruling 7), and the `NAME Type` 